[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: The board layout keeps a focal node between incoming and outgoing ranks

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-BOARD-LAYOUT
TITLE: The board sends only a bounded neighborhood to ELK.js
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
layout.js first traverses only the selected node's filtered incoming and outgoing
adjacency to the requested hop depth, ranks candidates for deterministic
clipping, and retains at most 48 nodes. Only that bounded induced subgraph is
sent to the vendored ELK.js 0.12 worker; the whole canon is never serialized to
the layout engine.

ELK Layered places the measured card boxes from left to right and returns
orthogonal edge sections. The board supplies measured relation-label boxes and
label-label, label-node, edge-edge, edge-node, and inter-layer spacing, then
translates ELK positions, bends, and label coordinates back into
application-owned render objects. Fan-outs of at least three semantic edges
sharing an endpoint, role, and direction use an invisible junction: one labeled
trunk branches into unlabeled terminal pieces. Generic ELK edge merging stays
disabled because it is not role-aware. Every routed piece retains its original
semantic edge objects so filtering, inspection highlighting, direction styles,
and counts continue to describe canon relations.

Layout is asynchronous. A monotonically increasing request token makes the
latest depth, filter, hash, or refresh selection win and discards stale worker
results. Initial layout failures keep the app from reporting ready; later
failures reach the shared error banner. The API and worker are pinned,
same-origin assets served through the static allowlist.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The ELK.js layout replaced the reference implementation's custom SCC, barycentric, and orthogonal router on 2026-09-02. elkjs 0.12.0 is vendored under its EPL-2.0 OR GPL-3.0-or-later license.
<<<
